window.docContents[232].resolve({"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Time-Regularity-for-Weak-Evolution--Time-regularization":{"contents":"For , convolution in time with the normalized\none-dimensional mollifier sends a locally integrable Banach-valued function\non  to a smooth trajectory. If  for\n, the regularizations converge to  in that Bochner\n norm along every positive sequence .\nIf  is the weak time derivative of  on all of , thenThis approximation identity is the mechanism behind the Lions–Magenes\nenergy identity.\n\nApply Banach-valued mollifier theory to the functions on , using\nlocal integrability for smoothness and global  membership for norm\nconvergence. Applications on a finite interval require a compatible\nextension or cutoff before this global derivative identity applies. To\nidentify the derivative, test the convolution against a continuous linear\nfunctional, move the scalar derivative from the mollifier onto the weak\nderivative by the distributional identity, and use separation of points in\nthe Banach space. This yields\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation\u0009Time Regularity for Weak Evolution","header":"6.3.1.6.1. Time regularization","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Time-Regularity-for-Weak-Evolution--Time-regularization"},"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Regularity___-random-media___-and-fluid-flow":{"contents":"Scott Armstrong and Julia Kempe's formalization of De Giorgi–Nash–Moser theory\nproves regularity from a weak formulation\n. For uniformly elliptic divergence-form\nequations with bounded measurable coefficients, their development in dimensions\nat least three establishes local boundedness, weak Harnack and Harnack\ninequalities, and interior Hölder regularity. The mathematical gain is the passage\nfrom a weak formulation to pointwise regularity without assuming smooth\ncoefficients. Their supporting Sobolev and weak-solution infrastructure overlaps\nwith the foundations needed throughout PDE formalization.\n\nArmstrong and Kuusi's CoarseGraining development extends this direction to\nquantitative stochastic homogenization\n. Its public comparison\ntheorem is stated for uniformly elliptic, isotropic coefficient laws. Their\nSuperdiffusion formalization treats results from work with Bou-Rabee on random\nincompressible flows ,\nfor a model with specified properties.\n\nFor Navier–Stokes, Tomoki Uda's Leray–Hopf project reports global weak-existence\ntheorems for the unforced equation on the three-dimensional torus and on\n, together with energy inequalities\n. Its public weak formulation uses\nseparated tests , and its existence theorems are for weak\nsolutions.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Related Work","header":"2.3. Regularity, random media, and fluid flow","id":"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Regularity___-random-media___-and-fluid-flow"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Compactness-and-Lower-Semicontinuity":{"contents":"A convex functional that is lower semicontinuous in norm is weakly lower\nsemicontinuous. Strict convexity adds uniqueness; existence does not need it.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009The Direct Method","header":"9.3.1.2. Compactness and Lower Semicontinuity","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Compactness-and-Lower-Semicontinuity"}});